cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  dallas-naloxone-access: measured block-level naloxone access and
  placement analysis for Dallas County, Texas
message: >-
  If you use this software or its derived data, please cite both the
  repository and the accompanying paper.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Ali
    name-particle: I.
    family-names: Ilyas
    email: research@savespots.org
    affiliation: SaveSpots National Harm Reduction Program
  - given-names: Sameer
    family-names: Ilyas
    email: research@savespots.org
    affiliation: SaveSpots National Harm Reduction Program
repository-code: "https://github.com/Ilyas1434/dallas-research-savespots"
abstract: >-
  An open, reproducible pipeline that measures the straight-line distance from
  every populated 2020 census block in Dallas County, Texas to every fixed
  naloxone access point, builds a five-layer tract-level composite
  vulnerability index with disclosed weights and reported sensitivity, and
  ranks candidate storefronts by need multiplied by measured reach among
  currently unserved residents. Because Texas mortality confidentiality rules
  suppress overdose death counts at fine geography, access is treated as the
  primary measured quantity and modelled mortality as one input among five.
keywords:
  - harm reduction
  - naloxone access
  - opioid overdose
  - spatial accessibility
  - data suppression
  - geographic health disparities
